---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-16"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Technology · Fundamentals · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Batteries in Series vs Parallel: Voltage and Capacity Wiring</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two wiring patterns answer almost every multi-battery question, and they do opposite things: one raises
        the voltage, the other raises the capacity.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Data summary:</strong> <strong>Series</strong> wiring (positive to negative) <strong>adds
        voltage</strong> — two 12V batteries make 24V at the same amp-hours. <strong>Parallel</strong> wiring
        (positive to positive, negative to negative) <strong>adds capacity</strong> — two 100Ah batteries make
        200Ah at the same voltage. Series changes the voltage; parallel changes the capacity.
      </div>

      <h2>The two rules</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Wiring</th><th>Result</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Series</strong></td><td>Voltage adds, capacity stays</td><td>2 × 12V 100Ah → 24V 100Ah</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Parallel</strong></td><td>Capacity adds, voltage stays</td><td>2 × 12V 100Ah → 12V 200Ah</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Series serves higher-voltage systems (24V trucks, 48V banks); parallel serves longer runtime at the same
        voltage. Combined series-parallel banks serve both at once — the building block of every large
        installation.
      </p>

      <h2>The matching rule</h2>
      <p>
        A bank is only as good as its <strong>weakest battery</strong>, so batteries in a bank should be
        <strong>matched</strong> — same chemistry, type, capacity and age. In series, an old battery's higher
        resistance forces it to overcharge while the others lag; in parallel, a weak battery drags the healthy ones
        down. Mixing old and new is the most common bank-killing mistake.
      </p>
      <h2>Worked example: a 2S2P pack</h2>
      <p>
        Four 3.2V 100 Ah <a href="/lithium/lfp-vs-nmc-vs-lto/">LFP</a> cells in a <strong>2S2P</strong> arrangement (two series pairs in parallel) produce
        <strong>6.4V</strong> and <strong>200 Ah</strong>: series doubles the voltage, parallel doubles the
        capacity. The same four cells in 4S give 12.8V and 100 Ah — the same total energy, a different system, and
        the 12.8V pack is the one a 12V installation actually uses.
      </p>

      <h2>What imbalance does to a pack</h2>
      <p>
        Series packs charge and discharge through every cell, so the pack follows its <strong>weakest cell</strong> —
        one lagging cell caps the whole string (see
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/cell-balancing/">cell balancing</a>). The matching rule
        exists for exactly this reason: mixed ages, capacities or chemistries build the imbalance in at assembly.
      </p>


      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My position:</strong> The series-vs-parallel rules are two sentences — voltage adds in series,
          capacity adds in parallel — but the real lesson is the matching rule, because a bank fails at its weakest
          battery, not its average.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Supporting logic:</strong> Wiring mistakes are rare; mismatch mistakes are everywhere. Once you know the two
          rules and that banks must be matched, you can build and diagnose any multi-battery system — and avoid the
          old-plus-new pairing that quietly ruins most banks.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between series and parallel batteries?</h3>
      <p>Series adds voltage: two 12V batteries wired in series make 24V at the same capacity. Parallel adds capacity: two 100Ah batteries wired in parallel make 200Ah at the same voltage. Series changes the voltage; parallel changes the amp-hours.</p>
      <h3>Can you mix old and new batteries in a bank?</h3>
      <p>Not advised. A bank is limited by its weakest battery — a new battery paired with a worn one is dragged down by it, and in series the imbalance causes one battery to overcharge. Banks should use matched batteries of the same type, age and capacity.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Is the bank wired in series (voltage adds) or parallel (capacity adds), or a deliberate combination?</li>
  <li>Do all batteries in the string match in chemistry, capacity, age and state of charge?</li>
  <li>Is the wiring sized for the current, with balanced cable lengths to avoid imbalance?</li>
  <li>Would a mismatch in the string cause the imbalance and early failure the wiring rules warn about?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Series adds voltage and parallel adds capacity, but both demand matched batteries — chemistry, capacity and age — or imbalance ages the pack unevenly. Wire by the two rules, match the cells, and the pack delivers what the arithmetic promises.</p>

